    "description": "/rewind undoes Claude's edits. But per the Claude Code docs, checkpoints are built for quick recovery within a session: a file that only a bash command changed isn't tracked. For lasting version history, git is still the tool.",
    "companion_text": "Follow Promptwerk for more Claude workflows, step by step. Based on the Claude Code docs (Checkpointing, Best practices); the terminal scene is an animated view, not a screen recording, and the prompt and file names are example values. Limits per the docs: once Claude has edited a file with its file editing tools, /rewind also undoes later bash changes to that file. Subagent edits, manual changes outside Claude Code and edits from other sessions are normally not captured. Claude Code deletes file snapshots about 30 days after the session last saved one by default (cleanupPeriodDays).",
